From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:08:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/smmstore: Check a write split at the end of a
 block as one

The payload's variable driver writes a variable header with one write
to its flash driver for each flash block, so a header that crosses the
end of a block arrives as two raw SMMSTORE writes. After the first one
the store ends in half a header, which coreboot's reader does not read:
every option would be read as its default at the next boot, and the
request of the MOR lock as absent. The MOR lock and the option lock
therefore refuse the piece, and the variable write of the OS fails,
there and at every later try, as the driver starts again at the same
place: about one append in a thousand, with either lock.

Keep such a piece instead: a write that the locks refuse and that ends
where a flash block ends is neither written nor failed, but kept in
SMRAM. When the next raw write begins where it ended, the two are
checked and written as the one write they are; any other write, or an
erase, drops the piece. Nothing reaches the flash that the locks have
not allowed as a whole, and while a lock is armed the flash no longer
holds the half header between the two writes, where a power loss would
have reset every option.

The copy of a checked write is now one buffer for both locks.

TEST=tests/drivers/smmstore_options_lock-test, test_split_header: the
piece kept and the flash unchanged, the header written whole with the
second piece and the variable completed; a piece dropped by another
write; two pieces refused together; an option's record still refused
when it becomes a variable.

Change-Id: If5a94798f3a9bf77777fb80b7de840922e390522
---
